<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pueblo peoples</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Pueblo peoples are Native American communities of the south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, chiefly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-mexico">New Mexico</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arizona">Arizona</a>, who live in compact villages of stone and adobe called pueblos. Descended from the Ancestral Puebloans, they speak several unrelated languages, including Tanoan, Keresan, Zuni and Hopi. Under Spanish rule from 1598 they were subject to tribute, forced labour and missionary pressure, which provoked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pueblo-revolt">Pueblo Revolt</a> of 1680.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pueblo-peoples.jpg" alt="Pueblo peoples" width="320" height="393" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Department of the Interior · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Secretary_Deb_Haaland,_official_headshot.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Pueblo is a Spanish word meaning 'village' and 'people', first used by Spanish explorers for the peoples' style of dwelling. In the twentieth century archaeologists often called the ancestors of the Pueblo peoples Anasazi, a word introduced by Alfred V. Kidder from a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/navajo">Navajo</a> term meaning 'enemy ancestors'. Contemporary Pueblo people object to it as derogatory, and the term Ancestral Puebloan is now preferred.</p><p>Pueblo societies contain elements of three cultures that dominated the Southwest before European contact: the Mogollon, whose people lived near the Gila Wilderness; the Hohokam; and the Ancestral Puebloan culture of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaco-canyon">Chaco Canyon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesa-verde">Mesa Verde</a> regions. The first evidence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a> cultivation in the Southwest dates from about 2100 BCE. By about 700 to 900 CE people had begun to leave pit houses for connected rectangular rooms of adobe, and by 1050 they had built planned villages of large terraced buildings.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pueblo-bonito">Pueblo Bonito</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaco-canyon">Chaco Canyon</a>, the largest of these villages, held around 700 rooms in five stories and may have housed as many as 1,000 people. The Tiwa people fought the Coronado Expedition in the Tiguex War of 1540–41, and a group led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-de-onate">Juan de Oñate</a> arrived in 1598. Spanish efforts to replace Pueblo religion with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a> grew steadily harsher.</p><p>In the 1670s a severe drought brought famine and more frequent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apache">Apache</a> raids. Growing resentment of Spanish abuses led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pueblo-revolt">Pueblo Revolt</a> of 1680, the first uprising led by a Native American group to expel colonists from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a> for a considerable time. In 1970 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> returned the 48,000 acres of Blue Lake mountain land to Taos Pueblo.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Pueblo peoples speak languages of four distinct families, so that speakers of one are not easily understood by speakers of another. Keresan is spoken at Acoma, Laguna and several other pueblos; Tanoan includes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tewa">Tewa</a>, Towa at Jemez and the Tiwa languages; Hopi is spoken on the Hopi Reservation in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arizona">Arizona</a>. Communities are organised around family clans. Corn is the staple food. The Zuni and Hopi practise dry farming, while the River Pueblos irrigate.</p><p>Many Pueblo people hold beliefs in Katsinas, supernatural beings regarded as representatives of the ancestors, who bring rain and can heal or cause disease. Pueblo communities have preserved much of their culture, including a syncretic Pueblo Christianity. About 75,000 Pueblo people live chiefly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-mexico">New Mexico</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arizona">Arizona</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pueblo peoples” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Ancestral Puebloans are the ancestors of the <b>Pueblo peoples</b> of Arizona and New Mexico: the Hopi, the Zuni, the people of Acoma and Laguna and the pueblos of the Rio Grande.</blockquote><a href="/culture/ancestral-puebloans">Ancestral Puebloans · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Oral history also recalls a long relationship with the <b>Pueblo peoples</b> and a readiness to take in Puebloan ideas.</blockquote><a href="/culture/navajo">Navajo · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Pueblo peoples</b> kept New Mexico free of Spanish rule for twelve years.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-21">21 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Governors ruled from the Palace of the Governors, while Franciscan missions sought to convert the <b>Pueblo peoples</b>, who were burdened with tribute and forced labour under encomienda.</blockquote><a href="/state/santa-fe-de-nuevo-mexico">Santa Fe de Nuevo Mexico</a></li></ul></section>
